ependent functionality to be gracefully handled or skipped when state is disabled.\" So a page with on_load → compiled without state; the on_load event handler wouldn't be wired to the backend (no socket). That's \"gracefully skipped\".\n\nNow the ReflexRuntimeError check in `_compile`: \"Catch 'static' apps (that do not define a rx.State subclass) which are trying to access rx.State.\" If state is disabled, this error would fire for any component that touches state contexts. Should we keep raising or skip? The user says pages that \"appear to use state features\" should compile without state support. But a component that directly references `rx.State.foo` genuinely needs state — compiling it without state support would produce broken JS (undefined `StateContexts`). Hmm.\n\nBut wait — where does the \"$$typeof Context is undefined\" error come from? Let me think: In a static app (no state), `compile_contexts(None, theme)` renders CONTEXT without `StateContexts`. If a page still imports `StateContexts` (e.g., via stateful components code, or the `state_name` kwarg), the browser throws \"TypeError: can't access property '$$typeof', Context is undefined\" because `StateContexts` import resolves to undefined... Actually the import would fail at module resolution. Hmm, the error arises when `Context` (from react) is undefined — e.g. `react.createContext` called with undefined... \n\nActually, I recall this issue: https://github.com/reflex-dev/reflex/issues/… \"TypeError: can't access property '$$typeof', Context is undefined\" occurs in Firefox when a component uses `useContext(Context)` where Context is undefined. In Reflex, this happens when the compiled page references `StateContexts` but the contexts.js module doesn't export it (because state was never enabled — e.g., when running `reflex export`/frontend-only or when the page uses `Upload` or `connection_pulser` etc. that reference state contexts without the app having state).\n\nSpecifically: `default_overlay_component` includes `connection_pulser()` and `connection_toaster()` which use state hooks — but app_wraps only include Overlay when stateful (line 362). Hmm.\n\nActually the known issue: with `rx.Config(state_manager_mode=...)`? No. Let me search git log / GitHub for the error string. Not critical — the user's ask is clear: add `enable_state` config flag. This actually matches a real upstream feature? Let me check upstream reflex for \"enable_state\" in config... I don't think it exists upstream. But there IS `config.enable_state`? Hmm. Actually I do not recall. Let me quickly search GitHub via WebSearch later if needed. Actually, better to check if the repo's docs mention it. Let me just check the docs folder for \"disable state\".\n\nActually, hold on. Let me check `reflex/utils/prerequisites.py` or `reflex/reflex.py` (CLI) for anything state-related at init, and also check `pyi_generator`/`_compile` for `compile_contexts`. And importantly — the `state.py` `State.setup_dynamic_args` in `add_page` (line 831): when state is disabled, should we skip `state.setup_dynamic_args(get_route_args(route))`? Route args (dynamic route params like `[key]`) become state vars... wait, do they? `setup_dynamic_args` on State sets dynamic args as class vars on State so pages can access route params as vars. With state disabled, pages can't use route params as vars — but the dynamic args setup also matters for the router props? Let me check `setup_dynamic_args`.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Z.AI","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7764e7ad-7165-4849-a6ff-d2cf0980932e","uuid":"20baa1c1-787e-4c05-8795-4ca34efe2d5d","timestamp":"2