{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_40aafc7d7beb4d9b88afd85f","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"reflex-dev reflex config \"enable_state\" disable state management app config\"\n\nHere's what the search found regarding Reflex's `enable_state` option:\n\n## Summary of Results\n\n### 1. **`enable_state` App Parameter** ([Reflex App API Reference](https://reflex.dev/docs/api-reference/app/))\nThe `rx.App` class accepts an `enable_state` parameter:\n- `enable_state: bool = True` — \"Whether to enable state for the app. If False, the app will not use state.\"\n\n### 2. **Background: PR #5583 — \"disable stateful app detection\"** ([GitHub](https://github.com/reflex-dev/reflex/pull/5583))\n- **Merged July 17, 2025** (author: adhami3310)\n- Previously, Reflex **automatically detected** whether an app used state by analyzing components and event triggers\n- This PR removed automatic detection and introduced the **explicit `enable_state` flag** on the `App` class, making state handling more predictable\n- It also fixed a `TypeError: can't access property \"$$typeof\", Context is undefined` that occurred when state was implicitly disabled but still being used\n- Key changes: added `enable_state` boolean to `App` (default `True`), removed automatic state detection during page compilation\n\n### 3. **v0.8.3 Release Notes** ([newreleases.io](https://newreleases.io/project/github/reflex-dev/reflex/release/v0.8.3))\n> \"Disable stateless app detection — If you have a stateless app, now you need to explicitly set `rx.App(enable_state=False)` for it to be truly stateless.\"\n\n### 4. **How It Works Internally** ([reflex/app.py source](https://github.com/reflex-dev/reflex/blob/3c114519/reflex/app.py))\n- In `__post_init__`: `if self.enable_state: self._enable_state()` — which sets `self._state = State` and sets up the state manager, Socket.IO server, etc.\n- When `enable_state=False`, no state manager is created (accessing `app.state_manager` raises `ValueError`)\n- App wraps respond to the flag: e.g., the default overlay component and `on_error` handler are only included when stateful\n\n### Usage Example\n```python\nimport reflex as rx\n\n# Stateful app (default)\napp = rx.App()\n\n# Stateless app — disables state entirely\napp = rx.App(enable_state=False)\n```\n\n**Note:** `enable_state` is an argument to the `rx.App()` constructor in your main app module — it is **not** an option in `rxconfig.py` (the config file). If you were looking for a config-level setting, there doesn't appear to be one; this is controlled per-app via the constructor.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7764e7ad-7165-4849-a6ff-d2cf0980932e","uuid":"aabe1402-855a-447b-9405-1d1d3c86909d","timestamp":"2026-09-04T08:17:17.392Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"reflex-dev reflex config \"enable_state\" disable state management app config","results":["Here's what the search found regarding Reflex's `enable_state` option:\n\n## Summary of Results\n\n### 1. **`enable_state` App Parameter** ([Reflex App API Reference](https://reflex.dev/docs/api-reference/app/))\nThe `rx.App` class accepts an `enable_state` parameter:\n- `enable_state: bool = True` — \"Whether to enable state for the app. If False, the app will not use state.\"\n\n### 2. **Background: PR #5583 — \"disable stateful app detection\"** ([GitHub](https://github.com/reflex-dev/reflex/pull/5583))\n- **Merged July 17, 2025** (author: adhami3310)\n- Previously, Reflex **automatically detected** whether an app used state by analyzing components and event triggers\n- This PR removed automatic detection and introduced the **explicit `enable_state` flag** on the `App` class, making state handling more predictable\n- It also fixed a `TypeError: can't access property \"$$typeof\", Context is undefined` that occurred when state was implicitly disabled but still being used\n- Key changes: added `enable_state` boolean to `App` (default `True`), removed automatic state detection during page compilation\n\n### 3. **v0.8.3 Release Notes** ([newreleases.io](https://newreleases.io/project/github/reflex-dev/reflex/release/v0.8.3))\n> \"Disable stateless app detection — If you have a stateless app, now you need to explicitly set `rx.App(enable_state=False)` for it to be truly stateless.\"\n\n### 4. **How It Works Internally** ([reflex/app.py source](https://github.com/reflex-dev/reflex/blob/3c114519/reflex/app.py))\n- In `__post_init__`: `if self.enable_state: self._enable_state()` — which sets `self._state = State` and sets up the state manager, Socket.IO server, etc.\n- When `enable_state=False`, no state manager is created (accessing `app.state_manager` raises `ValueError`)\n- App wraps respond to the flag: e.g., the default overlay component and `on_error` handler are only included when stateful\n\n### Usage Example\n```python\nimport reflex as rx\n\n# Stateful app (default)\napp = rx.App()\n\n# Stateless app — disables state entirely\napp = rx.App(enable_state=False)\n```\n\n**Note:** `enable_state` is an argument to the `rx.App()` constructor in your main app module — it is **not** an option in `rxconfig.py` (the config file). If you were looking for a config-level setting, there doesn't appear to be one; this is controlled per-app via the constructor."],"durationSeconds":23.675116714000005,"searchCount":1}}
