tains `useContext(StateContexts` — i.e., components that actually render state vars (not merely \"appear to\"). That's a deliberate hard error in upstream (kept). I'll keep upstream behavior — matching upstream exactly is the right call, and silently compiling a component that references `StateContexts.name` when the state doesn't exist would produce exactly the `$$typeof` TypeError the user wants to avoid. So keeping that guard actually protects requirement \"prevent runtime errors\".\n\nWait, actually, hmm: with `enable_state=False` and NO state subclass defined at all, does that error path even matter? `StatefulComponent.compile_from(page)` extracts stateful subcomponents. If a component references `rx.State.foo` but state is disabled... The var data has state references → memoized into StatefulComponent → uses `useContext(StateContexts.state_name)`. With state disabled, `compile_contexts(None, ...)` renders `StateContexts = {}` → `StateContexts.name` is undefined → the exact TypeError. So raising ReflexRuntimeError at compile time is the graceful handling (compile-time error instead of runtime browser error). Keep it.\n\nNow — one more thing to check: the upstream diff's test change in `test_app_wrap_compile_theme` and `test_frontend_exception_output` (the second big hunk) relies on the current repo's test expectations matching. It applied cleanly, so the context matched.\n\nBut hold on — the second hunk (line ~1435 area) — which test is that? Let me view the modified tests to be sure they're coherent. Also, since the old repo's `__post_init__` called `_setup_state()` (a no-op when `_state` is None), many OTHER tests may now behave differently because `App()` now always enables state. E.g., tests that create `App()` and assert `_state is None`, or count state subclasses, or check `_stateful_pages`. The upstream diff updated `test_app_state_determination` and `test_app_state_manager`. But there may be other tests in the repo (not touched by the PR) that break — t